"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9950],{9950:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"orthopaedics-test-1","slug":"orthopaedics","subject":"Orthopaedics","testNo":1,"durationMin":29,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 30-year-old male patient presents with progressive swelling of his right wrist associated with a dull aching pain. The orthopedician elicits tenderness on firm palpation of the swelling. The following findings are seen on X-ray. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":["Chondrosarcoma","Osteosarcoma","Osteoclastoma","Osteochondroma"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Osteoclastoma\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis in this patient is osteoclastoma/giant cell tumor.\\n\\nOsteoclastoma is a bone tumour with varying growth potential. It can be either benign/malignant, though it is more commonly benign. It is seen in patients who are 20-40 years of age. The commonly affected sites are the lower end of the femur, the upper end of the tibia, and the distal end of the radius.\\n\\nPatients present with swelling and vague pain. Pathological fractures may also be seen. On examination, firm palpation can elicit tenderness.\\n\\nRadiologically, soap bubble appearance is seen due to interspersed lytic lesions and bone remnants.\xa0Intralesional curettage of the tumour is the common approach to management.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Chondrosarcoma\xa0is a malignant bone tumour arising from the cartilage cells. It may arise in any bone but is common in flat bones such as the scapula, pelvis, and ribs.\\n\\nOption B: Osteosarcoma\xa0is a highly malignant primary bone tumor. Common sites are the lower end of the femur, the upper end of the tibia, and the upper end of the humerus. Patients present with a history of pain followed by swelling. Radiologically, periosteal reaction, sunburst appearance, and Codman\'s triangle can be seen. The characteristic sunburst appearance can be seen below.\\n\\nOption D: Osteochondroma\xa0is a common benign tumour of the bone. The tumour stops enlarging in size once there is a cessation of growth at the epiphyseal plate. It is usually seen in adolescents who present with painless swelling around a joint, usually the knee. X-ray shows a well-defined exostosis\xa0as shown below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/7e4f71eeb41aefbfe203.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/11f8e54ed05185a6f939.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e32090b4599d3b9c268b.jpg"}]},{"n":2,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"The knee deformity shown below is called as _____","options":["Genu varus","Genu recurvatum","Genu valgum","Windswept deformity"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Genu valgum\\n\\nThe deformity shown is genu valgum.\\n\\nA normal knee has 5-7 degrees physiological outward deviation. Any increase or decrease in angle can result in knee deformities.\\n\\nKnee deformities are:\\n\\n• Genu valgus (valgum)\xa0or knock knee - Knees are abnormally approximated and the ankles are divergent.\\n• Genu varus (varum)\xa0or bow knee -\xa0Knees are abnormally divergent \xa0and the ankles are approximated.\\n• Windswept deformity - Abnormal valgus deformity of one knee with varus of the other.\\n• Genu recurvatum - Hyperextension at knee joint, knee bends backwards.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/043818254e9ec1a0a2e4.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b1b483acd1cf697230f2.jpg"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 40-year-old patient presented to the emergency department following a road traffic accident. The radiograph of his pelvis is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Anterior dislocation of the hip","Posterior dislocation of the hip","Central dislocation of the hip","Fracture shaft of femur"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Posterior dislocation of the hip\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis of the condition given in the picture is posterior dislocation of left hip joint.\\n\\nThe radiograph shows the following findings:\\n\\n• The femoral head is displaced superiorly and laterally\\n• The left femoral head appears slightly smaller than the right femoral head\\n• The limb is adducted and internally rotated","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/98b3204f578e1e9d0092.png"]},{"n":4,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"An elderly male has pain and swelling in the knee joint. Synovial fluid on polarized light microscopy demonstrated","options":["Positively birefringent needle shaped crystals","Negatively birefringent rhomboid shaped crystals","Negatively birefringent needle shaped crystals","Positively birefringent rhomboid shaped crystals"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Positively birefringent rhomboid shaped crystals\\n\\nThe given case is highly suggestive of pseudogout and in the study of synovial aspirate on polarized light, the crystals appear as Positively birefringent rhomboid shaped crystals\\n\\nCrystals on Polarised Microscopy\\n\\nGout\\nPseudogout\\n\\nNegatively Birefringent\\nPositviely birefringent\\n\\nNeedle Shaped\\nRhomboid crystals\\n\\nNa Urate(Sodium Urate)\\nCa pyrophosphate","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"While examining a patient with a right forearm fracture, you ask him to make a fist. The following finding is noted during this procedure. What is the sign called as ?","options":["Froment\'s sign","Pointing index sign","Benediction sign","Policeman tip deformity"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pointing index sign\\n\\nThe given image shows the\xa0pointing index sign and is associated with\xa0median nerve injury\xa0at the forearm or elbow.\\n\\nThe\xa0pointing\xa0index\xa0sign is seen\xa0in\xa0supracondylar fractures\xa0of the humerus.\\n\\nThis finding occurs as a result of\xa0injury\xa0to the\xa0anterior interosseous nerve,\xa0a branch of the median nerve. It results in the loss of function of both flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) and flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS). Hence, the index finger sticks out when the patient is asked to clasp their hand.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0 In\xa0Froment\'s sign, due to the\xa0weakness of the adductor pollicis muscle,\xa0the adduction of the thumb is lost. When asked to hold an object, the patient tries to hold the object by using the\xa0flexor pollicis longus\xa0muscle. This produces flexion at the interphalangeal joint while holding the book.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0\xa0Paralysis of\xa0flexor pollicis brevis\xa0can occur in median nerve\xa0injury\xa0at the\xa0arm\xa0and\xa0elbow.\xa0Benediction hand sign\xa0(attempt to make a fist gives pointed index and middle finger) and\xa0Ochsner\'s clasping test\xa0(attempt to\xa0clasp both hands show\xa0pointing index finger) are used to test such injuries.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0\xa0Policeman tip deformity, also known as waiter\'s tip hand or porter\'s tip hand, is a finding of\xa0Erb\'s palsy\xa0due to injury of the\xa0upper trunk\xa0of the brachial plexus involving\xa0C5\xa0and\xa0C6. The hand is adducted, medially rotated, and hangs by the side with the elbow extended and pronated.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/5a19c7476b6d7fd7b09a.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/966d51191c9109404d62.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/da1fc557e2829209c0fd.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/83c6ad5d056e091e1885.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/100f47619c901f80d272.jpg"}]},{"n":6,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 6-year-old boy presented with the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Klippel-Feil syndrome","Long thoracic nerve injury","Sprengel deformity","Milwaukee shoulder"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sprengel deformity\\n\\nThe image shows an\xa0upward elevation of the scapula relative\xa0to the thoracic cage characteristically seen in Sprengel deformity.\\n\\nSprengel deformity is a congenital\xa0upward elevation of the scapula. It is\xa0due to the failure of the scapula to descend completely in embryonic life.\\n\\nThe affected scapula is smaller, shaped differently, abnormally high, and somewhat prominent. Shoulder movements are usually painless. Abduction and elevation of shoulders may be limited due to fixation of the scapula.\\n\\nThe\xa0Cavendish classification is used to grade the severity of Sprengel deformity.\\n\\nIf the deformity is mild, no treatment is required. In severe deformity, surgery may be indicated. These procedures include:\\n\\n• Green procedure\\n• Woodward procedure\\n• Mears procedure","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/b2949e470ca35d292804.jpg"]},{"n":7,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 67-year-old patient with a history of diabetes presented with swelling around the knee, restriction of movement, and minimal or no pain. The x-ray of the knee is given below. The diagnosis is _________.","options":["Rheumatoid arthropathy","Charcot arthropathy","Psoriatic arthropathy","Osteoarthritis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Charcot arthropathy\\n\\nThe x-ray depicts severe joint destruction and instability, seen on (a) varus stress (b) neutral (c) valgus stress on the patient\'s knee joint. There is minimal pain and this\xa0indicates a neuropathic cause like Charcot arthropathy.\\n\\nCharcot arthropathy is a neuroarthropathy commonly associated with diabetes.\\n\\nDiabetes is the most common cause of Charcot arthropathy, while tertiary syphilis was one of the common causes previously.\\n\\nThough the exact pathogenesis of this disease is unclear, it is speculated that\xa0vascular,\xa0neurological, and\xa0metabolic changes\xa0associated with diabetes lead to this condition.\\n\\nRheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, and psoriatic arthropathy present with pain.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/734676942226fbfff0c5.jpg"]},{"n":8,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with a gradually progressing swelling and pain for 6 months over the upper tibia. His radiographic image is shown below. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Osteosarcoma","Osteoclastoma","Ewing\'s sarcoma","Brodie\'s abscess"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Brodie\'s abscess\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a young boy having gradually progressive pain and swelling and the radiographic image showing a well-defined lytic lesion with a dense sclerotic rim are most suggestive of Brodie\'s abscess.\\n\\nBrodie\'s abscess is a localized form of subacute osteomyelitis that occurs most often in the long bones of the lower extremities of young adults. The organism most involved would be Staphylococcus aureus. It usually presents with intermittent pain\xa0that lasts long and local tenderness over the affected area.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Immobilization and antibiotics (flucloxacillin and fusidic acid) intravenous for 4-5\xa0days, followed by oral administration for another 6\xa0weeks; healing may take up to 12\xa0months\\n• Curettage is needed if the X-rays show no healing after conservative management.\\n• Curettage is also done along with open biopsy in doubtful cases.\\n\\nAll of the given conditions may present with a history of swelling and pain. However, the differentiating features of each of the given conditions are as follows:\\n\\nCondition\\nAge group\\nRadiographic findings\\n\\nBrodie\'s abscess\\n\\nChildren, adolescents\\n\\nA lytic lesion with a dense sclerotic rim, minimal periosteal reaction\\n\\nOsteosarcoma\\nChildren, adolescents, and seventh decade\\nPeriosteal reaction leading to sunray appearance and Codman triangle\\n\\nOsteoclastoma\\n20-40 years\\nA lytic lesion with a thin sclerotic rim, minimal periosteal reaction\\n\\nEwing\'s sarcoma\\n10-20 years\\nPeriosteal reaction leading to onion peel appearance","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/89b4488b130e13b54948.jpg"]},{"n":9,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 45-year-old patient presents to your clinic with complaints of stiffness of the joints in their fingers for the past 6 years that has progressively worsened over time. On examination, the following deformity is noted. What is the diagnosis in this case?","options":["Osteoarthritis","Gout","Rheumatoid arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Rheumatoid arthritis\\n\\nThe given image shows a swan-neck deformity seen in a patient with established rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Swan neck deformity is hyperextension at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint and flexion at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint.\\n\\nRheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic inflammatory disease of unknown etiology characterized by a symmetric polyarthritis and is the most common form of chronic inflammatory arthritis.\xa0A variation in the the HLA-DRB1 gene is known to cause RA. Cigarette smoking is known to increase the risk five to six fold.\\n\\nHistopathological features include\xa0synovial cell hyperplasia and proliferation\xa0with\xa0inflammatory infiltrates\xa0and\xa0rheumatoid nodules.\xa0These are\xa0necrotizing granulomas\xa0surrounded by\xa0fibrinoid necrosis.\\n\\nRA is a systemic disease, which potentially leads to a variety of extraarticular manifestations, including fatigue, subcutaneous nodules, interstitial lung disease and pulmonary nodules, pericarditis, peripheral neuropathy, vasculitis, and hematologic abnormalities, which must be managed accordingly.\\n\\nClinically, patients present with early morning joint pain and stiffness that relieves with activity. It mainly involves the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints. Other hallmark features include flexor tendon tenosynovitis, swan neck deformity, boutonniere deformity,\xa0Z-line deformity, and trigger finger.\\n\\nPatients may have\xa0elevated ESR\xa0(erythrocyte sedimentation rate)\xa0and\xa0C reactive protein\xa0(CRP).\xa0Rheumatoid factor\xa0and\xa0anti-CCP\xa0(cyclic citrullinated peptide) antibodies are helpful in prognosis. Progression of joint damage is assessed by plain X-ray.\\n\\nManagement comprises of\xa0non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs\xa0for pain relief, along with disease modifying anti-rheumatoid drugs (DMARD).\xa0Methotrexate\xa0is the DMARD of choice.\xa0Hydroxychloroquine\xa0(HCQ) is given as an adjuvant in combination with other DMARDs.\xa0Biological agents\xa0such as TNF-α inhibitors, anakinra and rituximab are known to reduce the signs and symptoms of RA, slow radiographic progression of joint damage, and improve physical function and quality of life.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A-\xa0Osteoarthritis(OA)\xa0typically affects the weight-bearing joints like the knee, hip, and spine. It presents bilaterally with\xa0pain\xa0being the predominant symptom. The pain worsens with use. Other symptoms such as joint stiffness, swelling, giving away or locking can be present.\\n\\nOption\xa0B-\xa0Gout has typical features of pain, swelling, and warmth of the first metatarsophalangeal joint. The radiograph is normal in the initial stages while it shows\xa0erosions, punched-out lesions,\xa0and\xa0sclerosis\xa0with preservation of joint space in the late stages.\\n\\nOption C-\xa0Psoriatic arthritis\xa0is an\xa0inflammatory musculoskeletal disease\xa0characteristically occurring in\xa0individuals\xa0with\xa0psoriasis.\xa0\xa0Psoriatic arthritis\xa0of the hands commonly involves proximal and distal interphalangeal joints (PIP,\xa0DIP) and metacarpophalangeal joints (MCP).\xa0The distal interphalangeal joint (DIP) is the most common joint involved in psoriatic arthritis. X-ray shows a characteristic pencil-in-cup appearance.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/c980030fbb4674421255.jpg"]},{"n":10,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A patient presents to the emergency department after he fell on his outstretched hand. An X-ray was performed and is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Colle\'s fracture","Galeazzi fracture","Rolando\'s fracture","Montegia fracture"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Galeazzi fracture\\n\\nThe\xa0 X-ray reveals\xa0an oblique fracture in the lower third of the radius and there is dislocation of the distal radio-ulnar joint, which is diagnostic of\xa0Galeazzi fracture-dislocation.\\n\\nThe patient with Galeazzi fracture-dislocation presents with pain, swelling and deformity at the lower end of the radius.\xa0On clinical examination, the disruption of the radio-ulnar joint can be elicited by pressing the lower end of the ulna. The ulna moves down and comes back like the key of a piano (piano-key sign).","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/15783d0873b593b52ac3.jpg"]},{"n":11,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A 55 year old female complains of lower backache. The radiographic image of her lumbosacral spine is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":["Renal osteodystrophy","Ankylosing spondylitis","Osteomalacia","Osteoporosis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Osteoporosis\\n\\nThe above image depicts\xa0ballooning of disc spaces\xa0leading to\xa0biconcave shape of vertebral body\xa0known as\xa0‘Cod\xa0fish appearance’,\xa0classically seen in\xa0osteoporosis.\\n\\nIt is caused due to the reduction in the strength of the bone that leads to an increased risk of fractures. WHO defines osteoporosis as a T score\xa0less than -2.5. It is the\xa0gold standard to assess bone mineral density.\\n\\nOther radiological features of osteoporosis:\\n\\n• Loss of vertical height\xa0of vertebra due to collapse\\n• Ground glass appearance of bones\xa0especially in the bones like pelvis\\n• Loss of cortical bone (picture frame vertebrae) and trabecular bone (ghost vertebrae)\\n• Loss of trabeculae in proximal femur area.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/5849c8e0a8106e30345f.png"]},{"n":12,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A patient was admitted to ICU 48 hours after the fracture of the femur. The saturation of oxygen in the rebreathing unit was 100% but his Spo2 remained 60%. The patient is in a state of confusion. Chest radiograph showed lung fields to be clear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Fat embolism","Pulmonary embolism","ARDS","Occult pneumothorax"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fat embolism\\n\\nThe most likely diagnosis of the above clinical scenario is a fat embolism. Presence of respiratory insufficiency and altered mental status following a long bone fracture (fracture of the femur) favours the diagnosis.\\n\\nFat embolism syndrome results from the presence of numerous fat emboli within the microcirculation. It most commonly occurs after the fracture of the long bone or pelvis. They\xa0present with non-specific symptoms like tachypnea, tachycardia and fever that\xa0manifest within 24- 48 hours after the injury. Damage to the microvasculature of various organs leads to :\\n\\n• Mild hypoxia to rapidly progressing respiratory failure.\\n• Depression of the central nervous system.\\n• Petechial rashes\\n• Hemorrhagic lesions of the retina.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A patient who had applied plaster to his right leg for a tibial shaft fracture ,now presents with severe pain in the leg. On examination, the distal pulses are absent, on passive stretching the pain increases, and on opening the plaster, the leg appears edematous and numerous blisters are seen. Considering the suspected diagnosis, what would be the most appropriate management in this case?","options":["Closed reduction","Fasciotomy","Opening of the plaster is sufficient","Elevation of limb"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fasciotomy\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario suggests acute compartment syndrome, and it is treated immediately with fasciotomy.\\n\\nThe classic features of ischemia in acute compartment syndrome include the 5 Ps:\\n\\n• Pain:\xa0Pain is the first symptom of acute compartment syndrome. Ischemic muscle is highly sensitive to touch. On the passive stretching of the muscles, severe pain can be elicited.\\n• Paresthesia:\xa0Paresthesia and hypoesthesia may be noted due to compression of nerves traversing the affected compartment. They are the first signs of nerve ischemia.\\n• Pallor: Pallor of the skin may not always be seen. The skin outside the compartment may be normally perfused, as only the intra-compartmental perfusion may be affected.\\n• Paralysis:\xa0Paralysis of muscle groups affected in that compartment is a late sign.\\n• Pulselessness:\xa0Usually, the peripheral pulses and\xa0capillary return are intact in the early stages, unless there is a major arterial injury. If pulses are absent, arteriography is indicated.\\n\\nThe diagnosis can be confirmed by measuring the intra-compartmental pressure\xa0using a split catheter introduced into the compartment. The difference between diastolic pressure and compartment pressure is known as the\xa0differential pressure (ΔP). A\xa0differential pressure (ΔP) of less than\xa030 mm Hg (4.00 kilopascals) is an indication for immediate compartment decompression.\\n\\nTreatment: The threatened compartment must be promptly decompressed, merely splitting the plaster is utterly useless and the limb must be nursed flat, elevating the limb causes further decrease in end capillary pressure and aggravates the muscle ischemia. In the case of compartment syndrome in the leg, Fasciotomy means opening all four compartments. Wounds to be left open and inspected 2 days later, if tissues are healthy, the wounds can be sutured. If there is muscle necrosis, debridement to be carried out.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A patient has a history of a road traffic accident 2 years back, and has developed pain and swelling now at the same site. His X-ray image is shown in the image given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":["Osteogenic sarcoma","Multiple myeloma","Ewing\'s sarcoma","Chronic osteomyelitis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Chronic osteomyelitis\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and the X-ray image are suggestive of\xa0chronic osteomyelitis of the tibia.\\n\\nChronic osteomyelitis may occur by direct inoculation of the infective organism during trauma (like road traffic accident). The infection continues to thrive within the bone leading to alternating quiescent and flare up stages of chronic osteomyelitis.\\n\\nThe radiographic image shows oval-shaped sequestrum and periosteal new bone formation surrounding the sequestrum known as\xa0involucrum.\\n\\nThe characteristic radiographic features of other conditions given are:\\n\\nOsteogenic sarcoma - Sun-burst appearance, Codman\'s triangle\\n\\nEwing\'s sarcoma - Onion skin appearance\\n\\nMultiple myeloma - Punched out lesions","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/db08b7187d46804c2a19.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2bc0fb752b6040a46c48.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"A throw ball player presents to the emergency room with pain over the shoulder, arm abducted and externally rotated and loss of sensation over the proximal lateral aspect of the arm. Which of the following nerve injuries is likely associated with this condition?","options":["Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of arm"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Axillary nerve\\n\\nThe clinical presentation is suggestive of anterior dislocation of shoulder. The most common nerve injured in these cases is the axillary nerve as it winds around the humerus.\\n\\nIn axillary nerve injury, there is loss of sensation over a small part of the deltoid region in upper arm (regimental badge area) and weakness in abduction (15-90 degress) due to denervation of deltoid. Overhead abduction is unaffected as it is the function of serratus anterior and trapezius muscles.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"Identify the given condition:","options":["Osteosarcoma","Chondroblastoma","Ewings sarcoma","Adamantinoma"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Osteosarcoma\\n\\nThe given X-ray image showing the\xa0sunburst appearance\xa0is suggestive of\xa0osteosarcoma.\\n\\nOsteosarcoma\xa0is the\xa0most common primary malignant tumour\xa0of the\xa0bone, with\xa0bimodal age\xa0of distribution peaking in adolescence\xa0and during the 7th decade.\\n\\nThe most common site involved is the\xa0distal femoral metaphysis, which\xa0mainly presents with pain and swelling. Other sites commonly involved are the\xa0proximal tibia, proximal femur, and humerus.\\n\\nX-rays features include:\\n\\n• Codman’s triangle →\xa0due to new bone formation in response to periosteal elevation\\n• Sunburst appearance → due to\xa0new bone formation along the blood vessels within the tumor\\n• Periosteal reaction → due to the lifting of the periosteum of the bone. Periosteal reaction in osteosarcoma is irregular, unlike in osteomyelitis where it is smooth.\\n\\nWide excision\xa0and reconstruction are preferred\xa0if\xa0the tumour is\xa0resectable\xa0and there are no skip lesions. It is done after starting\xa0neoadjuvant chemotherapy\xa0for 8-12 weeks.\xa0Chemotherapeutic agents used are doxorubicin, cisplatin, ifosfamide, and methotrexate.\\n\\nEwing\'s sarcoma\xa0is the second most common primary malignancy\xa0of bone after osteosarcoma. It usually occurs at a younger age and commonly involves the\xa0diaphysis\xa0of the long bones like the femur, tibia, fibula, and humerus. Most tumors have a balanced translocation (11;22) involving EWSRI gene and FL1 gene.\\n\\nOnion-peel appearance\xa0due to deposition of periosteal reaction in layers is seen in radiology (as shown below) and Homer-Wright rosettes, which are round groupings of cells with a central fibrillary core may be seen histologically.\\n\\nChondroblastoma is a benign cartilaginous tumour occurring mainly in long bones. On X-rays, they appear as a well-circumscribed, round, or oval lytic lesion in the centre of the epiphysis of long bones with areas of calcification within the tumour. On histology presence of \\"chicken wire calcification\\" is pathognomic.\\n\\nThe image below shows an X-ray with\xa0a well-circumscribed, round lytic lesion in the centre of the epiphysis of the tibia suggestive of chondroblastoma.\\n\\nAdamantinoma\xa0is a\xa0rare malignant\xa0tumour. Most cases appear in the lower extremities and classically in the\xa0tibial diaphysis. It is most often seen in the second to fourth decade of life. The patient usually presents with pre-tibial pain and swelling.\xa0X-ray shows\xa0multiple sharply demarcated radiolucent lesions\xa0in the\xa0tibial diaphysis.\xa0Microscopy shows\xa0epithelial cells in a fibrous stroma.\xa0Intralesional or marginal excision causes local recurrence so\xa0complete excision of the tumour\xa0is the treatment of choice.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/2c00949b2a73cc517f63.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/72bdc8683d37e281099e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/12e04e758afae11deb9a.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c195bfe2e3c4586a6dfe.jpg"}]},{"n":17,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"Which of the following tendons is most commonly affected in the shoulder impingement syndrome?","options":["Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Teres major","Supraspinatus"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Supraspinatus\\n\\nSupraspinatus\xa0is the most commonly affected tendon in\xa0shoulder impingement syndrome.\\n\\nIt is also called\xa0supraspinatus syndrome,\xa0painful arc syndrome, and\xa0swimmer\'s shoulder\xa0and occurs when the tendons of the rotator cuff muscles become irritated and inflamed as they pass through the subacromial space. This can result in pain, weakness, and loss of movement at the shoulder.\\n\\nCauses of impingement syndrome:\\n\\n• Supraspinatus tendinitis (Common)\\n• Complete or partial rupture of the rotator cuff\\n• Osteoarthritis of the acromioclavicular joint\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Subacute tendinitis or painful arc syndrome: Anterior shoulder pain after vigorous activity. Treatment is with avoidance of initiating activity, NSAIDs and physiotherapy.\\n• Chronic tendinitis:\xa0Recurrent attacks of subacute tendinitis and pain is worse at night.\\n• Cuff disruption:\xa0There is progressive fibrosis and disruption of the cuff leading to a partial or full-thickness tear. Treatment includes acromioplasty and repair of rotator cuff.\\n\\nThe following video shows the action of the subscapularis through the subacrominal space:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e8ac332ea774c0f769d3.gif"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"Shepherd\'s crook deformity is seen in ______","options":["Osteoblastoma","Fibrous dysplasia","Non-ossifying fibroma","Osteoid osteoma"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fibrous dysplasia\\n\\nShepherd\'s crook deformity is seen in\xa0fibrous dysplasia of proximal femur.\\n\\nFibrous dysplasia is a developmental disorder in which area of trabecular bone is replaced by cellular fibrous tissue.\xa0If this occurs in the weight-bearing bones like the femur, it will lead to the bending of bones called as ‘Shepherd’s crook\' deformity.\\n\\nImage: Bending of upper femur in fibrous dysplasia known as Shepherd’s crook deformity","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/986b6209892363e52c15.png"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"Orthopaedics","text":"Which of the following statements is false about the condition shown in the image below?","options":["Less chances of recurrence","Transillumination positive","It can be surgically excised","It can be seen on the dorsal and volar aspect of wrist"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Less chances of recurrence\\n\\nImage A shows a ganglion cyst of the wrist and image B shows the surgical excision of the cyst. Image B shows a swelling that is cystic, translucent, and filled with a fluid which is suggestive of a ganglion cyst. It has high rates of recurrence and thus , option A is false.\\n\\nGanglion cyst:\\n\\n• It arises from the leakage of synovial fluid from a joint or tendon sheath and thus contains a glary, viscous fluid.\\n• It can appear anywhere in the wrist but the most common area is the dorsal surface of the scapholunate ligament.\\n• Usually appears as a painless, well-defined, cystic, transillumination positive, and non-tender swelling. If there is an impingement on a nearby nerve or structure it can cause pain.\\n• It disappears spontaneously, but the aspiration of the swelling or operative removal can be done for reassurance or when there is pressure on the nerve.\\n• If the swelling is near the radial artery or nerves then removal is done under ultrasound guidance.\\n• Even if treatment is done, the rates of recurrence are high.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nA lipoma can be a differential here but it is not a transilluminant swelling and has a positive slip sign, i.e when palpated, the edge of the swelling slips under the finger.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/b361d0471d5282555ab8.jpg"]}]}')}}]);